const enBody = `  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cartesia 2026: The Real-Time Voice API Built for AI Agents</h1>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What is Cartesia, and why does the Sonic TTS API matter in 2026?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Cartesia is a San Francisco-based voice AI lab that built Sonic — a real-time text-to-speech (TTS) model designed from the ground up for conversational AI agents, voice assistants, and latency-sensitive customer-interaction workloads. As of June 2026, the company has raised $87M across a Series A (June 2024, led by Index Ventures at $50M) and a Series B (March 2026, led by Lightspeed at $37M), and the API at <code>api.cartesia.ai</code> is the only first-party path to the Sonic model family. The co-founders — Karan Goel, Luis Pinto, and Albert Gu — came out of Stanford's Hazy Research lab (the same group behind FlashAttention and the Mamba state-space model family), and Sonic inherits the same low-latency, high-throughput design philosophy that made FlashAttention a default transformer kernel.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The reason the Sonic TTS API is worth a dedicated review in July 2026 is that voice-agent architectures shifted decisively from "stitched-together TTS + LLM + STT" to "single low-latency stream" over the past 18 months. The bottleneck is not the LLM (Claude Opus 4.5 streams tokens in 80-120ms TTFB), nor the STT (Deepgram and AssemblyAI both cross 1-second audio processing in under 300ms). The bottleneck is the TTS round-trip — the time from "LLM finishes generation" to "first audio sample arrives at the user's ear." In a phone-call voice agent, anything above 500ms total latency makes the conversation feel turn-by-turn; below 300ms feels like a real human. Cartesia Sonic's headline number is 90ms TTFB on the Sonic model — the lowest in the market by a 3-4x margin versus ElevenLabs Flash v2 (250-400ms TTFB) and OpenAI TTS-1-HD (300-500ms TTFB).</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">That 90ms number is the central reason Cartesia is on the apirank.vip 2026 radar. For a startup building a customer-support voice agent, a real-time language tutoring app, or an in-game NPC dialogue engine, the TTS round-trip is the make-or-break latency floor. A 400ms TTS turns a "natural-feeling" conversation into a "phone-tree" experience. Cartesia Sonic's combination of low latency, multilingual support (29+ languages in Sonic 2), and a WebSocket streaming interface makes it the de-facto choice for voice-agent builders in 2026 — and the focus of this review.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">This review covers Cartesia from the perspective of an engineer evaluating it in July 2026: the six endpoints in the Sonic API (Sonic, Sonic 2, Onyx, Mistral, Pleroma, TTS-Streaming), the per-character and per-second pricing model, the 90ms TTFB latency profile, the voice cloning flow (5-second sample to clone any voice), the MCP server for Claude Code and Cursor integration, and how Cartesia compares to ElevenLabs, Deepgram, OpenAI TTS, and LMNT for the same real-time voice-agent workloads.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Cartesia endpoint catalog in 2026</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The Cartesia API exposes six models in 2026, organized into three families — text-to-speech (TTS), speech-to-text (STT), and voice design. Each family has tiered quality/price options, and the API exposes all of them through the same WebSocket and HTTPS surface at <code>https://api.cartesia.ai</code>. The Sonic endpoint is the flagship low-latency TTS; Sonic 2 is the multilingual variant with voice cloning built in. Onyx is the STT model, Mistral is the "voice design" model that lets you describe a voice in natural language and get a sample back, and Pleroma is the dedicated voice-cloning endpoint. TTS-Streaming is the WebSocket streaming interface that wraps all of the TTS models and is the recommended path for any real-time voice-agent integration.</p>

