  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The pricing structure is character-based for TTS, hour-based for STT, and per-sample for voice design. There is no subscription lock-in — every endpoint is pay-as-you-go with a 10,000 characters/month free tier that does not require a credit card. The character count is the input text length, not the audio duration, which means a 1-hour TTS output consuming 50,000 characters of input costs around $1.50 on the Sonic Standard tier.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Sonic deep dive: the 90ms TTFB voice model</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Sonic is Cartesia's flagship TTS model, and the 90ms time-to-first-byte (TTFB) is its defining technical claim. For context, "TTFB" in TTS means the time between the API receiving the start of a text stream and the first audio sample arriving back at the client. This is the single most important number for any voice-agent builder because it determines the "feel" of the conversation. Below 200ms feels real-time; above 400ms feels turn-by-turn. Cartesia's Sonic-Standard tier delivers 90ms TTFB on English text in the most common configuration (PCM 24kHz, opus mode, 80-character chunks). The Pro tier trades a 30ms latency increase for higher audio quality (44.1kHz), and the Turbo tier pushes quality further at 200-220ms TTFB.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The architecture underlying the latency is a state-space model (SSM) design — the same family that Mamba belongs to — rather than the autoregressive transformer design that ElevenLabs Turbo v2.5 and OpenAI TTS-1 use. SSM-based sequence models have O(n) inference scaling with the input length, compared to the O(n&sup2;) attention scaling in transformers, which means the per-step inference cost is dramatically lower. Cartesia's published benchmarks show Sonic running in 90ms TTFB on a single A100 GPU with sub-2% WER (word error rate) on English benchmarks like LibriSpeech. The architectural choice also explains why Sonic is competitive on price: at $0.03 per 1K chars on the Standard tier, the marginal inference cost is roughly 30-40% below what an equivalent transformer-based TTS would charge.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For voice-agent builders, the practical implication of the 90ms TTFB is that the entire call-stack latency budget (LLM token streaming + TTS round-trip + network + WebRTC) can land in the 400-600ms range that feels like a real conversation. With ElevenLabs Flash v2 (250ms TTFB) the same architecture would land at 600-900ms — a slow but functional conversation. With OpenAI TTS-1-HD (350-500ms TTFB) it is typically 800ms-1.2s, which feels turn-by-turn. The voice-agent "feel" budget is tightly bound to TTS latency, and Sonic is the only commercial API that puts the round-trip below 200ms in the default tier.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Sonic 2: multilingual + voice cloning in one model</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Sonic 2 is the follow-up model launched in February 2026, and the headline additions are native support for 29+ languages (vs Sonic's English-best with partial multilingual fallback) and a built-in voice cloning path that accepts a 5-30 second sample of the target voice. The latency profile is slightly higher than Sonic (140ms TTFB vs 90ms) because the multilingual codec requires extra processing headroom, but the quality gain is significant — Sonic 2's multilingual voices score 0.36 MOS (mean opinion score) points above the original Sonic on non-English benchmarks, and the natural-language voice cloning is the first commercial path that does not require uploading a 5-minute training sample.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For a builder shipping a multilingual voice agent — say, a customer-support bot that needs to switch between English, Spanish, and Mandarin based on the caller's language — Sonic 2 is the only commercial API in 2026 that delivers sub-200ms TTFB across all three languages from a single endpoint. The alternative architectures typically require either a translation step (adds 200-500ms) or a per-language model swap (adds cold start overhead of 1-3 seconds). Sonic 2 keeps the conversation in a single continuous stream.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The pricing for Sonic 2 is $0.04-$0.15 per 1K characters depending on quality tier — roughly 30-50% above Sonic's pricing. For a single-language English voice agent, Sonic Standard is the better economic choice. For any multilingual workflow, the additional cost is more than recovered by eliminating the translation step. The voice cloning path on Sonic 2 is also the simplest in the market: you upload a 5-second audio clip, wait 3-5 seconds for the clone to register, and start using the new voice ID in your TTS requests. The clone itself is free (no per-clone fee on Sonic 2); the only cost is the per-character TTS rate.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cartesia pricing: how the character billing works in practice</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Cartesia uses a character-based pricing model for TTS, which is unusual for the voice API market — ElevenLabs and OpenAI both use the same model, but most STT-first vendors (Deepgram, AssemblyAI) use an hour-based audio model. The practical implication is that voice-agent cost is a function of the LLM's output token volume, not the length of the conversation. For a typical customer-support agent that emits 200 characters per turn on average, a 1,000-turn conversation costs roughly $6 on Sonic Standard ($0.03/1K chars * 200 chars = $0.006 per turn * 1,000 turns). Wait — that is $0.006 per turn, so the 1,000-turn conversation costs $6 total. Cheap enough that voice-agent economics in 2026 are not bottlenecked by TTS.</p>

