  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The free tier is generous for prototyping but is no-cost, not a credit — once you exceed 10,000 characters/month, billing kicks in at the pay-as-you-go rate. There is no subscription plan for the per-character TTS (unlike ElevenLabs' $5-$330/month tiered plans), which means the bill scales linearly with usage. For a startup in growth, this is the right model — you do not have to forecast capacity to choose a tier. For an enterprise with predictable volume, the lack of a committed-use discount is a real con (ElevenLabs' Scale plan offers 30-50% volume discounts on annual commit).</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How Cartesia compares to ElevenLabs, OpenAI TTS, and Deepgram</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The voice API market in 2026 has four credible contenders for a TTS-focused stack: ElevenLabs (quality leader), Cartesia (latency leader), OpenAI TTS (bundled-with-GPT-4o), and Deepgram (STT-first with growing TTS). For a voice-agent builder, the choice comes down to whether latency or voice-quality aesthetics is the binding constraint. ElevenLabs Flash v2 produces the highest subjective voice quality (MOS 4.42 vs Sonic's 4.18 on internal benchmarks), but it has 250-400ms TTFB. OpenAI TTS-1 has bundled billing with GPT-4o but 350-500ms TTFB. Cartesia Sonic has the lowest latency (90ms TTFB) at the cost of slightly lower subjective quality.</p>

