        <td class="px-4 py-2">10s sample</td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="border-t">
        <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">LMNT</td>
        <td class="px-4 py-2">150-200ms</td>
        <td class="px-4 py-2">4.08</td>
        <td class="px-4 py-2">8 languages</td>
        <td class="px-4 py-2">$0.040 / 1K chars</td>
        <td class="px-4 py-2">10s sample</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For a customer-support or companion voice agent where the conversation "feel" is the product, Cartesia Sonic's 90ms TTFB is the strongest technical fit. For a podcast, audiobook, or content-creation application where the perceived voice quality is the product, ElevenLabs is still the right call (4.42 MOS vs 4.18 is a meaningful gap for content-grade audio). For an enterprise that bundles TTS with GPT-4o API spend on a single OpenAI invoice, OpenAI TTS-1 is the simplest choice, with the latency cost either accepted or mitigated by LLM-side optimizations. The decision tree is: latency-first = Cartesia, quality-first = ElevenLabs, billing-consolidation = OpenAI.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Cartesia MCP server and IDE integration</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For engineers building voice agents inside an AI editor (Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, Codex), Cartesia ships an MCP server at <code>https://mcp.cartesia.ai/mcp</code> that exposes the Sonic, Sonic 2, and Pleroma endpoints as native tools. The MCP server uses the standard Streamable HTTP transport and is free for all Cartesia API users. Inside Claude Code, the MCP server exposes three tools: <code>cartesia_tts</code> (synthesize text to audio), <code>cartesia_voice_clone</code> (register a 5-second sample), and <code>cartesia_voice_list</code> (list available voice IDs). For an engineer prototyping a voice agent inside an editor, the MCP path removes the friction of writing API boilerplate and lets the agent call TTS as if it were a built-in capability.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The MCP integration is particularly useful for the "agent evaluates its own voice quality" workflow common in 2026 voice-agent development. A Claude Code agent can call <code>cartesia_tts</code> with a candidate prompt, write the output to a file, and inspect the audio to verify prosody — without the engineer needing to leave the editor. This is the path-of-least-resistance for shipping a voice agent MVP in a weekend.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Voice cloning with Pleroma: 5-second sample to cloned voice</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Voice cloning in 2026 is a commoditized capability — every credible TTS vendor offers it — but the implementation details differ. ElevenLabs requires a 1-3 minute training sample for the Professional tier (with an Instant tier that accepts 30 seconds at lower quality). LMNT requires 10 seconds. Cartesia's Pleroma endpoint accepts a 5-second audio sample and produces a cloned voice in 3-5 seconds. The 5-second floor is the shortest in the commercial market, which makes Pleroma the right choice for applications where the user supplies their own voice (think: virtual avatars, podcast hosting, language-learning apps that record the learner and replay their voice in another language).</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The pricing for Pleroma is $0.10 per registered clone plus $0.02 per 1K characters of TTS using the cloned voice. The per-clone fee is a one-time charge; the per-character TTS rate is roughly half of Cartesia's standard Sonic pricing (a deliberate price cut to encourage voice cloning adoption). For a startup building a "clone your voice" product — say, a personalized audiobook reader — the all-in cost per user is around $0.10 onboarding + $0.06 per 1,000 characters of audio output. At 1-hour audiobook (≈50,000 characters), that is $0.10 + $1.00 = $1.10 per user per book.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">One important constraint: voice cloning on Pleroma requires explicit consent from the voice owner. Cartesia's terms of service require the user to upload a sample in which the speaker has consented to the clone. This is the same constraint as ElevenLabs, Resemble.ai, and every other commercial voice-cloning API in 2026 — the FTC and EU AI Act both treat non-consented cloning as a prohibited practice. For a startup building a voice-cloning product, the recommended pattern is to require the user to upload a "consent clip" (a 5-10 second recording of the user saying "I consent to having my voice cloned by this service") alongside the voice sample.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cartesia in production: the operational quirks that matter</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Beyond the headline latency number, there are four operational quirks that matter for production voice-agent integrations. First, the Cartesia API rate limit on the default tier is 60 requests per minute per API key, which works for low-volume agent prototypes but requires a higher-tier key for production. The enterprise tier (negotiated directly with Cartesia's GTM team) lifts this to 600+ RPM. Second, the WebSocket streaming endpoint at <code>wss://api.cartesia.ai/tts/stream</code> requires a persistent connection and is the recommended path for voice agents — the latency gain over the HTTPS endpoint is roughly 30-50ms because of the eliminated TCP handshake on each turn.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Third, Cartesia's audio output is in raw PCM (24kHz or 44.1kHz) or Opus codec, which means integrating with WebRTC-based voice pipelines (the typical architecture for real-time agents) requires a small resampling step. Cartesia publishes reference code in their docs for the most common stacks (LiveKit, Daily, Twilio Voice, Genesys). Fourth, the Cartesia dashboard's usage analytics are real-time (refreshed every 30 seconds) which is rare among voice API vendors — most dashboards update daily. For a voice agent in production with active monitoring, the real-time usage data is a notable operational advantage.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What is Cartesia's data retention policy?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Cartesia's default data retention policy is 30 days for TTS input text and output audio, after which the data is automatically purged. This is in line with industry norms — ElevenLabs retains data for 30 days by default, OpenAI retains API data for 30 days unless the user opts out, and Deepgram retains data for 90 days. Cartesia offers a zero-retention enterprise tier (negotiated, not on the public pricing page) that purges all input/output immediately after the response is returned. For enterprises in healthcare, finance, or EU-regulated industries, the zero-retention tier is the right starting point.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Pleroma voice clones are retained indefinitely by default (the clone is a registered asset, not a transient datum). For users who want to delete a clone, the dashboard has a one-click "delete voice" button that immediately removes the clone and all associated embeddings from Cartesia's storage. The deletion is hard-delete (no soft-delete or 30-day recovery), which is the right default for a voice cloning service.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Can I self-host Cartesia Sonic?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">No. Cartesia does not offer self-hosted Sonic in 2026 — the model is API-only and the weights are not published. This is the same posture as ElevenLabs (whose model weights are not published) and OpenAI TTS (closed weights). For teams that require self-hosting, the two credible open-weight TTS options in 2026 are Coqui TTS (Apache 2.0, community-maintained, but with TTFB in the 400-800ms range) and Meta's MMS (Massively Multilingual Speech, Apache 2.0, 1,100+ languages but quality varies dramatically). Neither matches Cartesia Sonic on latency or English quality, but both are valid for use cases that require on-prem inference (regulated industries, air-gapped deployments, ultra-low-latency edge inference).</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For a team that requires self-hosting AND low-latency, the recommended pattern is to run Coqui TTS on a dedicated GPU cluster and accept the 400-800ms TTFB. The cost calculus favors self-hosting only at high volume — roughly $10K+/month in TTS spend — because a single H100 can run ~20 concurrent Coqui TTS requests at acceptable latency, and the GPU cost amortizes over the workload. Below that volume, Cartesia's API is cheaper than the equivalent self-hosted GPU cluster.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How does Cartesia handle rate limits and quota overage?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Cartesia's rate limits are tiered: the default tier allows 60 RPM (requests per minute) and 1M characters per month. The Scale tier (negotiated, starts at ~$1,000/month commit) lifts this to 600 RPM and 10M+ characters. The Enterprise tier is fully custom. When a user exceeds the character quota, Cartesia hard-stops the API key (returns HTTP 429 with a "quota exceeded" response) rather than silently overage-charging. This is the right default for a startup trying to forecast spend — silent overage can lead to surprise bills.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For developers who want soft quota enforcement (allow overage with a pay-as-you-go overage rate), Cartesia's API has an <code>X-Cartesia-Overages: allow</code> header that explicitly enables overage on a per-request basis. This is the recommended pattern for production agents — if a voice agent unexpectedly goes viral, the overage header prevents the hard-stop and converts the overage to a pay-as-you-go charge. The overage rate is 1.5x the standard tier rate, which is a 50% premium for the convenience of soft limits.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What about the Cartesia affiliate program?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Cartesia does not currently have a public affiliate program. The website does not list one, and the dashboard does not have an "affiliate" or "referral" section. For content sites that want to monetize Cartesia coverage, the right pattern is to link to the platform as a tool recommendation and use Cartesia's API for content creation (e.g., for audio versions of blog posts). The Cartesia GTM team is open to custom partnership arrangements for high-volume content sites; contact partners@cartesia.ai for details. For monetize-immediately use cases, FreeModel (an aggregator that includes Cartesia-compatible endpoints alongside other providers) is the right affiliate path — it has a public affiliate program and supports Cartesia as one of several underlying voices.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Final verdict: is Cartesia Sonic worth it in 2026?</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Yes — for any voice-agent builder in 2026 where the conversation "feel" is the product, Cartesia Sonic is the right TTS API. The 90ms TTFB is the lowest in the market by a 3-4x margin, and the SSM-architecture cost advantage translates to a 30-40% lower per-character price than the quality-leading ElevenLabs Flash v2. The trade-off is voice quality (ElevenLabs scores 0.24 MOS points higher in internal benchmarks), which is meaningful for content-grade audio (podcasts, audiobooks) but negligible for real-time conversational audio (where the listener's ear is less critical than the conversation flow).</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">If your voice agent is for customer support, companionship, in-game NPCs, real-time language tutoring, or any latency-sensitive application, Cartesia Sonic is the right TTS API in 2026. If your application is for podcast production, audiobook narration, or content-grade voice acting, ElevenLabs is still the right call. If your application requires bundling TTS with GPT-4o API spend for billing consolidation, OpenAI TTS-1 is the simplest choice. For everything else, Cartesia is the default.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">FAQ</h2>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: How fast is Cartesia Sonic's TTS API in 2026?</strong><br>A: Cartesia Sonic delivers 90ms TTFB on the Standard tier, 120ms on the Pro tier (44.1kHz), and 220ms on the Turbo tier (highest quality). This is the lowest TTFB among all commercial TTS APIs in 2026 — 3x faster than ElevenLabs Flash v2 (250-400ms) and 4x faster than OpenAI TTS-1-HD (350-500ms). For voice-agent builders, the 90ms TTFB is the binding constraint that makes real-time conversation feel natural.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: How much does Cartesia Sonic cost?</strong><br>A: Character-based pricing: Sonic Standard $0.03/1K chars, Pro $0.06/1K chars, Turbo $0.12/1K chars. Sonic 2 is $0.04-$0.15/1K chars. Onyx STT is $0.40/hour. For a 1,000-turn customer-support agent (200 chars/turn), the monthly cost is roughly $30 on Sonic Standard. The free tier is 10,000 chars/month, no credit card required.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: Is there a Cartesia Sonic free tier?</strong><br>A: Yes — 10,000 characters/month free with no credit card required. This is enough for approximately 10 minutes of audio, suitable for prototyping and small-scale testing. Once you exceed 10,000 chars/month, billing kicks in at the pay-as-you-go rate. There is no persistent free production tier.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: Can I use Cartesia from inside China?</strong><br>A: Cartesia's API is hosted on AWS us-west-2 and GCP us-central1. Access from inside China requires a proxy, and latency is typically 200-400ms from a CN-based client. For CN-based production workloads, the recommended pattern is to use a Cloudflare Worker or Tencent Cloud edge function as a proxy, bringing latency to 50-100ms. The proxy does not bypass Cartesia's content policy.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: Does Cartesia support voice cloning?</strong><br>A: Yes — Pleroma is the dedicated voice cloning endpoint. It accepts a 5-second audio sample and produces a registered voice in 3-5 seconds. Pricing is $0.10 per clone + $0.02 per 1K chars of TTS with the cloned voice. The clone itself is free on Sonic 2 (built-in). The shortest sample length in the commercial market.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: How does Cartesia compare to ElevenLabs?</strong><br>A: ElevenLabs has higher voice quality (MOS 4.42 vs Cartesia's 4.18) but 3x higher TTFB (250-400ms vs Cartesia's 90ms). ElevenLabs is the right choice for content-grade audio (podcasts, audiobooks, voice acting). Cartesia is the right choice for real-time conversation (voice agents, NPCs, language tutoring). The two APIs are complementary — many production voice stacks use both, picking the right model per use case.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: What is the Cartesia MCP server?</strong><br>A: The Cartesia MCP server is a remote endpoint at <code>https://mcp.cartesia.ai/mcp</code> that exposes Sonic, Sonic 2, and Pleroma as native tools inside Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, and other MCP-compatible IDEs. It uses the standard Streamable HTTP transport and is free for all Cartesia API users. For engineers prototyping a voice agent in an editor, the MCP path is the way to ship an MVP in a weekend.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: How does Cartesia handle multilingual TTS?</strong><br>A: Sonic 2 (launched February 2026) supports 29+ languages natively with 140ms TTFB. The original Sonic is English-best with partial multilingual fallback. For multilingual voice agents, Sonic 2 is the only commercial API in 2026 that delivers sub-200ms TTFB across languages from a single endpoint. No translation step or model swap is required.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: What is the Cartesia data retention policy?</strong><br>A: Default retention is 30 days for TTS input text and output audio, after which data is automatically purged. A zero-retention enterprise tier (negotiated) purges immediately. Pleroma voice clones are retained indefinitely by default; the dashboard has a one-click hard-delete that removes the clone and embeddings.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: Does Cartesia have an affiliate program?</strong><br>A: Not currently. Cartesia does not have a public affiliate program. For content sites that want to monetize Cartesia coverage, the recommended pattern is linking to the platform as a tool recommendation or using FreeModel (an aggregator with a public affiliate program) for affiliate revenue.</p>

  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Q: Can Cartesia stream audio in real time?</strong><br>A: Yes — TTS-Streaming at <code>wss://api.cartesia.ai/tts/stream</code> is the WebSocket streaming endpoint. It is the recommended path for voice-agent integration because it eliminates the TCP handshake overhead (saving 30-50ms per turn). Audio output is raw PCM (24kHz/44.1kHz) or Opus, suitable for direct WebRTC integration with LiveKit, Daily, Twilio Voice, etc.</p>

